# What this repository is for, and what it deliberately is not.
#
# Everything Epoch *is* — the engine, the surfaces, the packs, the documents — is tracked. Three
# categories are not, and each for its own reason.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- builds
#
# 19 GB and reproducible from source. A build output in history makes every clone expensive and
# every diff unreadable, and it can always be rebuilt.
BUILD/target/
# A second target directory, used when something has a handle on the first one and the
# bundler cannot rewrite its NSIS staging. Same build, somewhere nothing is holding.
BUILD/target-pack/
BUILD/ui/dist/
BUILD/ui/node_modules/
# A vitest run from BUILD/ leaves its cache one level up from the one above.
BUILD/node_modules/
BUILD/crates/epoch-tauri/gen/schemas/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ credentials
#
# **Never tracked, in a private repository either.** A private repo is not a safe place for a
# secret: it is copied to laptops, restored from backups, and shared the day it stops being
# private. The rule is that a credential lives where it can be revoked, not where it is
# convenient.
#
# `agent-token` is the legacy plaintext bearer for the local MCP door — a key to a port that can
# run capabilities. It is migrated into DPAPI and deleted on the next opening of the door.
#
# `secrets.dat` is where it went, together with provider API keys: one store, encrypted for the
# logged-in Windows account. **It is a file, and this line is why.** The paragraph above used to
# say credentials never touch the filesystem at all — true when keys lived only in the DPAPI API,
# and no longer true once the store became a file in the vault. Nothing was tracked in the gap,
# but nothing was stopping it either: the working tree offered it for staging alongside the
# ordinary vault TOML around it. Encrypted-for-this-account is not a reason to carry a credential
# in history — it is copied to laptops, restored from backups, and shared the day the repository
# stops being private.
#
# **Kept even though `BUILD/vault/` below now covers both.** Redundancy is worth having exactly
# here: these two lines are what still stops a credential if somebody ever narrows that rule, and
# a rule that only holds while another rule holds is not a rule about credentials.
BUILD/vault/agent-token
BUILD/vault/secrets.dat
*.pem
*.key
.env
.env.*

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- the vault
#
# **Somebody's own Epoch, and never this repository's business.**
#
# A vault holds a person: their crew and what those characters are like, their conversations
# word for word, the paths to their real projects, the servers they connected, the permissions
# they have granted for good, and the encrypted store those credentials live in. None of it is
# what a user installs, and all of it was tracked — so a clone carried one developer's face,
# name, machine layout and standing decisions.
#
# This waited on two things, and both are now true.
#
# `paths.rs` already sends an **installed** build to `%APPDATA%\Epoch`, so nothing about the
# product depends on a vault sitting in the source tree; that location is a development
# convenience, which is exactly the kind of thing that should not be in history.
#
# And the Launcher's tests used to read this folder, which meant they measured whichever crew
# the developer happened to have — a test that a rename could turn red. They read
# `crates/epoch-engine/tests/fixtures/vault` now, which belongs to the tests and to nobody.
#
# What a *new* clone gets is an empty vault, and that is the correct first run: Epoch creates
# what it needs and the user brings their own crew. `packs/` and `assets/` stay tracked, because
# those genuinely are what ships.
BUILD/vault/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ generated schemas
#
# Codex's app-server writes its JSON-RPC schema here when Epoch asks what it can do. 136,000
# lines of somebody else's generated contract, regenerated on demand, and it belongs to the
# version of the CLI installed on this machine rather than to Epoch.
BUILD/.epoch-appserver-schema/

# ------------------------------------------------------------ somebody else's code
#
# Reference clones of other projects. They have their own history and their own licences, and
# vendoring them here would silently make Epoch's repository contain them.
REPOS/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- machine noise
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------- editors
#
# Obsidian's workspace state is where the panes were on one machine — noise in every diff.
.obsidian/workspace.json
.obsidian/workspace-mobile.json

# Throwaway capture from browser tooling. Timestamped noise, never read twice.
BUILD/crates/epoch-tauri/.playwright-mcp/

# Third-party README files, fetched when an MCP server is installed so the crew can read what its
# authors wrote about it. Somebody else's documentation, under somebody else's licence — it is a
# local cache of a public document, not content this repository distributes.
BUILD/vault/mcp-docs/

# The external audit reports. Owner's decision, 2026-09-08: they stay off the repository.
#
# They are evidence about a moment rather than part of the product, and the first one was
# committed before that was decided — `AUDIT-QA-2026-09-07.md` is untracked here in the same
# change that added this rule, and the file itself is left on disk.
#
# What each audit *found* is not lost with them: a finding that mattered is written into the
# code it changed, its test and its commit message, which is where somebody reading that code
# will actually be standing.
AUDIT-QA-*.md

# Visual Studio's own local state. Binary, per machine, and it carries the paths of whoever
# opened the solution last — it was tracked by accident rather than by decision.
BUILD/.vs/

# The Obsidian vault's configuration. The owner's editor, not the product: which plugins are on,
# where the graph view was left. It changes every time the vault is opened, which is why it was
# the one thing permanently dirty in `git status`.
.obsidian/

# Session handovers. Owner's decision, 2026-09-08, the same one the audit reports got.
#
# They are a conversation between one session and the next — true on the afternoon they were
# written and stale by the following morning. What survives from them belongs in the code, its
# tests and its commit messages, and `docs/Build/Handoff.md` is the one that is maintained.
CLAUDE_NEWPLAN.MD
CLAUDE_SUPERNEWPLAN.MD
CLAUDEtransfer.md
Claudetransfer8.md
LastClaudeTransfer.md
CODEX_NEWPLAN.MD
docs/Build/Minecraft.md
